00:37:10,220 --> 00:37:15,890
I do have sort of a standard list but I also defer to whoever I'm working with.

369
00:37:15,890 --> 00:37:20,120
so I typically say you should start by focusing on base salary.

370
00:37:20,120 --> 00:37:25,130
for me personally maximizing base salary rises above all else in terms of priority.

371
00:37:25,130 --> 00:37:33,680
and the reason is that you can pay your mortgage with base salary you can make a car payment with base salary you can't do that with stock options.

372
00:37:33,680 --> 00:37:38,180
and and so I usually start with base salary then there's paid vacation seems to be very popular.

373
00:37:38,180 --> 00:37:47,240
I like that as somebody who thinks about dollars and cents and negotiation because the paid vacation time does have an actual kind of real-world value.

374
00:37:47,240 --> 00:37:53,480
you know what a week of work is worth and so you know what the value of that vacation is.

375
00:37:53,480 --> 00:37:58,249
and it's also more or less pegged to your base salary so those are the first two.

376
00:37:58,249 --> 00:38:06,109
then signing bonuses and or relocation stipends for you know if I have to move across the country or relocate people like to negotiate on those.

377
00:38:06,109 --> 00:38:13,250
and then you've got other much smaller thing like you know getting your company to pay your cable bill or your cellphone bill.

378
00:38:13,250 --> 00:38:17,720
or if you work out of a co-working space getting them to pay that monthly lease amount.

379
00:38:17,720 --> 00:38:22,640
and this is all done in I call it the final discussion but it's it's after you've counter-offered and the company comes back.

380
00:38:22,640 --> 00:38:26,839
and says well we hear your counter we can't quite meet your counter here's what we're willing to do.

381
00:38:26,839 --> 00:38:30,049
and that's when you kind of start going back and forth seeing how far you can push your base salary.

382
00:38:30,049 --> 00:38:35,839
and then you work down you know the the first one or two things in your prioritized list of well if once I've maximized base salary.

383
00:38:35,849 --> 00:38:40,700
if I don't get to where I want to get what are the other things that are important to me that I'll go after.

384
00:38:40,700 --> 00:38:45,470
and like I said those are usually you know first is paid vacation and then we go from there.

385
00:38:45,480 --> 00:38:59,510
as you talked through those I was imagining a previous employer a particular boss and the fear that at some point he would say it's just not worth it.

386
00:38:59,510 --> 00:39:06,740
like I just we just don't need someone who's going to want to negotiate this hard and retract the offer actually came to mind.

387
00:39:06,740 --> 00:39:13,750
you said you don't see that happen very often is it a real problem and if am I the only one that fears that.

388
00:39:13,760 --> 00:39:22,789
it's a real problem in the sense that everyone is afraid of it so it's a it's a very common fear that people have that they're gonna negotiate.

389
00:39:22,789 --> 00:39:28,609
and then the company will say I just don't want to deal with this person nevermind offer off the table.

390
00:39:28,609 --> 00:39:36,470
so I think though that I can make a pretty compelling case that will help most people see why that fear is extremely unlikely to come to fruition.

391
00:39:36,470 --> 00:39:43,730
so I'm hedging a little bit I can't say for sure that you won't run into a particular recruiter who just really had a bad week.

392
00:39:43,730 --> 00:39:46,220
and they're just not interested in negotiating.

393
00:39:46,220 --> 00:39:52,099
but I have not experienced this and with anyone that I've coached I haven't seen it happen.

394
00:39:52,099 --> 00:39:59,390
right a lot of people have read my book I'm not getting you know emails for people saying I had a job offer retracted.

395
00:39:59,390 --> 00:40:04,520
I got one email from one person who said you say that companies won't retract the offer.

396
00:40:04,520 --> 00:40:09,770
if you negotiate I had an offer retracted when I asked some follow-up questions to understand their situation better.

397
00:40:09,770 --> 00:40:14,630
trying to say this diplomatically I'm not convinced that the reason their offer was retracted was that they negotiated.

398
00:40:14,630 --> 00:40:20,330
so that's one example out of I've sold hundreds of copies in my book right so it just doesn't happen.

399
00:40:20,330 --> 00:40:27,770
however people are afraid of it and this goes back to the overarching reason for fear and salary negotiations which is we don't get many opportunities to do this.

400
00:40:27,780 --> 00:40:31,460
you only get a few at-bats throughout your career you don't want to mess it up.

401
00:40:31,470 --> 00:40:37,460
and so anything that we perceive as something that could cause the a deal to evaporate is a very big deal for us.

402
00:40:37,470 --> 00:40:40,880
because that could be one of very few opportunities we have to get a good job.

403
00:40:40,890 --> 00:40:47,120
and so I think it's a legitimate fear that people have because we understand how people work.

404
00:40:47,120 --> 00:40:52,910
and we know that you know I personally would not like someone to negotiate against me if I offer them something.

405
00:40:52,910 --> 00:40:59,840
but salary negotiations are different they're unique we're trying to find that that happy medium.

406
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:07,100
so why don't I think that companies are prone to retracting job offers the short answer is back to the company's job of making a profit.

407
00:41:07,100 --> 00:41:12,860
dollars and cents are important to companies by the time you get a job offer from a company they have typically invested.

408
00:41:12,860 --> 00:41:17,150
if not thousands tens of thousands of dollars sometimes in recruiting you.

409
00:41:17,150 --> 00:41:25,370
and getting to the point where they have extended a real offer to you often just in man-hours they've usually burned thousands of dollars.

410
00:41:25,370 --> 00:41:31,610
so you've talked to a recruiter for an hour - you've had you know one two three up to a dozen interviews with various hiring managers.

411
00:41:31,620 --> 00:41:38,240
and sometimes people up to the you know SVP director c-level people those people's time is extremely valuable.

412
00:41:38,250 --> 00:41:45,980
they've invested resources and you know posting the job requisition and all these other things they're spending lots and lots of money.

413
00:41:45,980 --> 00:41:48,920
they might have flown you on site that could be thousands of dollars right.

414
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:55,520
and so you get to the point where they make you an offer they've invested significant money in getting to the point where they can make you that offer.

415
00:41:55,520 --> 00:41:58,760
and so from their perspective this is a pretty big investment that they're making.

416
00:41:58,760 --> 00:42:04,790
and so when they make you an offer they're not inclined to just basically light that investment on fire and get rid of it.

417
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:11,750
because you said you know you made me an offer I appreciate that I'd be more comfortable if we could do this other thing.

