00:50:57,359 --> 00:51:04,290
but a lot of larger enterprise companies will have created essentially a rubric that they'll use to decide.

513
00:51:04,290 --> 00:51:08,130
how much of a raise any one person in the company can get at a time.

514
00:51:08,130 --> 00:51:14,370
so I worked at a large enterprise company where the answer was if you move up one pay grade for a promotion you can get a 4% raise.

515
00:51:14,370 --> 00:51:20,730
if you move up to pay grades to get a to pay grade promotion you can get a seven and a half percent raise.

516
00:51:20,730 --> 00:51:26,910
anything above seven and a half percent it wasn't impossible but it was really hard to do without kind of moving mountains.

517
00:51:26,910 --> 00:51:29,490
and so that's something that really helped to be aware of.

518
00:51:29,490 --> 00:51:38,160
and hopefully your manager can tell you is are there strict constraints on the amount of a raise that someone can get in one move at this company.

519
00:51:38,160 --> 00:51:43,020
or is it all kind of handled ad hoc. is that okay to ask.

520
00:51:43,020 --> 00:51:47,940
yes usually you know if you're pursuing a raise or promotion you'll put together a case.

521
00:51:47,940 --> 00:51:53,700
and before you kind of send that written case you'll talk to your manager and have a conversation.

522
00:51:53,700 --> 00:51:58,799
and say here's what I'm my goal my goal for this job title that I'm pursuing.

523
00:51:58,799 --> 00:52:03,660
or this raise amount that I'm pursuing you know here's why I think I've earned that goal.

524
00:52:03,660 --> 00:52:09,150
can you tell me what we can do to make this happen and what sort of timeline we're looking at.

525
00:52:09,150 --> 00:52:12,359
and so part of that conversation would be hopefully collaborating with your manager to see what's available.

526
00:52:12,359 --> 00:52:21,509
and then finding out oh is there some sort of system in place a framework that prevents someone from getting an extraordinary raise at this company.

